Subject: A throw in the interpreter on a C-entered frame that has a caller returns to its C owner

What this does
  - `regvm_t_unwind_loop` tests `REGVM_FLAG_C_ENTRY` before `f->caller == NULL`, so a C-entered frame that has a caller ends the unwind and hands `JS_EXCEPTION` back to the C function that entered it.

Why we need it
  The frames compiled code builds for its callees (native entries, exit frames, inlined levels) are on the C stack, flagged C_ENTRY, and have a caller. The unwind loop took such a frame for an arena light frame: it computed the arena top from a C-stack address (a huge value stored in `regvm_frames_top`), freed the variable area, and continued in the caller. The next light call then allocated its frame at that bogus top.

Failure
  Not reachable until exits continue in the interpreter instead of the generated generic body (QJS_AOT_NOGENERIC): a TypeError thrown in the interpreter after an exit (null receiver in a native-method call from a region) crashed in `regvm_t_return` storing the next callee's result through `c->fr == NULL`. vcases r-native-this, r-construct, r-native-construct.

Soundness
  C-entered frames were already documented to be released by their C frame on both the return and the throw path; the test only moves ahead of the caller test.
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -34597,0 +34598,8 @@
+    /* A C-entered activation is released by the C frame that entered it, on
+       both the return and the throw path. The frame compiled code builds for its
+       callee has a caller, so the flag is tested first: such a frame is on the C
+       stack, not in the arena. */
+    if (unlikely(f->flags & REGVM_FLAG_C_ENTRY)) {
+        f->ret = JS_EXCEPTION;
+        return REGVM_C_RET;
+    }
@@ -34599,6 +34606,0 @@
-        /* A C-entered activation is released by the C frame that entered it, on
-           both the return and the throw path. */
-        if (unlikely(f->flags & REGVM_FLAG_C_ENTRY)) {
-            f->ret = JS_EXCEPTION;
-            return REGVM_C_RET;
-        }
